+## r3 root-control contract
+
+The r3 package authenticates the exact payload before any package-owned file
+can execute as root. The build writes a deterministic
+`conf/root-payload.manifest` containing the installed mode and SHA-256 digest
+of every regular file in the target payload, every package lifecycle script,
+the immutable privilege templates and the static package resource files. The
+manifest excludes itself and the mutable active `conf/privilege` file.
+
+The outer bootstrap performs promotion in this order:
+
+1. validate the DSM version, trusted ancestors, manifest syntax, inventory,
+   modes and file identities;
+2. stop the package through the bounded DSM package-control wrapper;
+3. create one private transaction under `conf/root-control/transactions`;
+4. replace each target file with a validated, root-owned, same-directory inode;
+5. repeat the complete no-follow target inventory and identity check;
+6. write bootstrap state bound to the manifest digest;
+7. install the root privilege template and start the package; and
+8. verify that `tailscaled` runs as UID 0 before removing the transaction.
+
+Privilege activation is deliberately last. Missing, extra, malformed,
+linked, unsafe-mode or identity-mismatched payload entries fail before root
+execution. A failed operation restores the prior privilege, target ownership
+and trusted bootstrap state when rollback can be proven. An incomplete
+rollback retains the single recovery transaction and blocks later mutations.
+
+The persistent downstream trust anchor is limited to:
+
+| Path | Required state | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `/var/packages/Tailscale/scripts` | DSM-installed `root:root`, not group/other-writable | authoritative lifecycle and bootstrap entrypoints |
+| `/var/packages/Tailscale/conf/root-payload.manifest` | `root:root 0644` | expected identities for the promoted payload |
+| `/var/packages/Tailscale/conf/root-control` | `root:root 0755` | bootstrap state namespace |
+| `.../root-control/root-bootstrap.state` | `root:root 0644` | schema, privilege mode and accepted manifest digest |
+| `.../root-control/transactions` | `root:root 0700` while active | private bounded rollback material |
+
+The controller rejects unexpected owners, types, links and write permissions
+on these paths. A package upgrade whose payload identity changes requires a
+new attended bootstrap. Legacy r2 bootstrap state beneath `SYNOPKG_PKGVAR` is
+not trusted as approval for an r3 payload.
+
+## Reconciliation and upstream compatibility
+
+Only the downstream reconciler's volatile control files move outside package
+data. Root lifecycle code validates or creates
+`/run/tailscale-synology` as `root:root 0700`; `reconciler.pid` and
+`repair.pending` live there. Package-account lifecycle and all status paths are
+observation-only and never create, repair or remove this runtime directory.
+Root stop removes exact control files only after process-identity validation.
+
+Reconciler messages use `/usr/bin/logger` with the stable
+`tailscale-netfilter-reconciler` tag and mapped system-log priorities. Logger
+failure is reported on stderr but does not create a package-data fallback log
+or turn successful firewall repair into a retry loop.
+
+The following upstream application paths and semantics remain unchanged:
+
+- `tailscaled.state`, `tailscaled.sock`, `tailscaled.pid`,
+  `tailscaled.stdout.log` and `STATE_DIRECTORY` remain beneath
+  `SYNOPKG_PKGVAR`;
+- the DSM web interface and LocalAPI continue to use the upstream socket;
+- daemon logrotate configuration remains unchanged;
+- the existing TUN, routing, sysctl and netfilter behavior remains unchanged;
+- iptables cleanup still checks whether a rule exists before deleting it and
+  requires observable progress within the deletion routine; and
+- bootstrap removal restores package-account execution and
+  `tailscale:tailscale` target ownership without moving or re-owning upstream
+  daemon state.
+
+This boundary intentionally retains the upstream package-data risk. A
+compromised package account can still alter application files it is expected
+to own beneath `@appdata`, including paths beneath predictable daemon PID and
+stdout-log ancestors. Executable-identity checks constrain process signalling,
+but eliminating all package-account influence over daemon state would require
+a broader package fork and is outside this port.
+
+## r3 acceptance contract
+
+Release acceptance requires two clean builds from the same signed source
+commit and fixed source epoch, with byte-identical sideload and Package Center
+SPKs. The static inspector must verify archive safety, metadata, script syntax
+and the complete root-payload manifest for every candidate.
+
+Attended DSM testing must use the recorded artifact digest and cover:
+
+- fresh install and upgrade from the accepted r2 package;
+- package-account pre-bootstrap status and rejection of linked mutations;
+- outer-script bootstrap, idempotent re-bootstrap, remove and re-bootstrap;
+- manifest-bound state, `root:root` target ownership and `tailscaled` UID 0;
+- package restart, NAS reboot, rollback, uninstall and volume migration;
+- DSM UI, LocalAPI and system-log visibility;
+- interrupted netfilter repair across same-boot restart and reboot; and
+- TUN, IPv4 and IPv6 netfilter, subnet routing, accepted routes and exit-node
+  operation.
+
+Build or static-inspection success does not authorise NAS installation,
+bootstrap, reboot or network mutation. Those remain attended UAT steps.
+
